I am building a small task list where items are added with JavaScript. Instead of adding a click listener to every button, I added one listener to the list itself. Each item has a delete button with a small icon inside it.

 When I click the text part of the delete button, the item is removed as expected. When I click directly on the icon inside the same button, nothing happens. There is no error in the console, and the item stays on the page.

 Here is a simplified version of what I have:

```

 Buy milk Delete 


```

```
const list = document.getElementById('taskList');

list.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
 const action = event.target.dataset.action;
 console.log(action);
 if (action === 'delete') {
 event.target.closest('li').remove();
 }
});

```

 What I tried:


 I checked that the listener is attached to the list, not to each button, because the items are added later.

 I logged `event.target` and saw it is different when I click the icon.


 Expected result: clicking anywhere on the delete button, including the icon, should remove the item.

 Actual result: clicking the button text removes the item, but clicking the icon logs `undefined` and does nothing.

 Why does the target change like this, and what is the reliable way to handle clicks on buttons that contain child elements when using event delegation?

solution:
Since Emma Collins' answer is already correct, this answer will focus on exploring alternative solutions. As she described, using closest will work in your case, because even though the event target is the exact element upon which the event has been triggered upon, calling `et.closest('button[data-action]')` will find the closest ancestor matching the criteria.


 EDIT 

 Since the other answer which was based upon the use of .closest() was removed, I'll elaborate on it. If the other answer is going to be undeleted, then we can remove this edit from here. The author of the now removed other answer was Emma Collins.

 So, basically the idea shared there was that .closest() is a function that takes a selector and starting from the element it is being called upon will repeatedly reach out to the ancestors of the node until it reaches a match of the selector. In the case of this question, this would look like




```
const list = document.getElementById('taskList');

list.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
 const closest = event.target.closest("[data-action]");
 if (closest) {
 let action = closest.dataset.action;
 if (action === 'delete') {
 event.target.closest('li').remove();
 }
 }
});
```

```

 Buy milk Delete 

```

 Let's discuss shortly what happens here. Just like in the original code, we create a click event on the list so clicks on descendants will propagate to the list and we can identify the element that was clicked via `event.target`. Now, the target of this event is the individual element that was clicked, which may or may not have a data-action. So we reach out to the first ancestor (including itself) that has a data-action. If the result is falsy then you have clicked on something that was not inside your button subtree, such as the Buy milk text and in such cases we don't need to do anything, as it's not an action button. Otherwise the click either occurred at the button or at the inner `svg` so we can infer and execute the `action` as planned.


 However, this solution has its limitations too. After all, if you add further action buttons, like edit, etc. etc., then reaching to the first descendant having a data-action may execute the wrong event, so in order to make her solution work even if you have multiple actions is to elaborate in the closest selector so the value is also specified. If you have many such buttons, it may become tedious.

 Of course, you could have an `onclick` for each elements and you will have no problems with adding elements there with Javascript if you have an onclick either,




```
function remove(that) {
 that.parentNode.remove();
}
```

```

 Buy milk Delete 
 Buy cheese Delete 
 Buy butter Delete 

```

 but your elements will be increasingly difficult to read if you have multiple events to handle. If it's `click` only, then it may be good-enough.

