 The `":input"` selector only works in jQuery and selects all form elements.

 Then with the collection of only form elements, `.serializeArray()` takes care of serialising their data to be transmitted. jQuery handles all the logic for what should be serialised and how - only selected radio buttons, for example.

 Since `.serializeArray()` returns data in the form of

```
[ 
 { name: "key1", value: "value1" }, 
 { name: "key2", value: "value2" } 
]

```

 it needs to be combined into a single object with `{ key1: "value1", key2: "value2" }`. - I chose to do it with `Object.fromEntries()` . It is a two-step transformation: each key-value object is converted to a key-value array `{ name: "key1", value: "value1" }` -> `["key1", "value1"]` then `Object.fromEntries()` consumes them to produce a single object.

 See How do I convert array of Objects into one Object in JavaScript? for other options, if needed.

 This is all handled by:

```
/** 
 * Convert any form fields to an object 
 * @param el - element where the form elements are. It does not need to be a form.
 * @returns {Object} - plain object representing the form elements names to values
 */
const formFieldsToObject = (el) =>
 Object.fromEntries(
 $(":input", el)
 .serializeArray()
 .map((formData) => [formData.name, formData.value])
 );

```

 From there `$("#DistanceTable tr.inputs")` selects each row with inputs and `Array.from` passes each of these rows through `formFieldsToObject`.

 If the form needs to support multiple values for each field, then `.serializeArray()` will produce data like

```
[ 
 { name: "key1", value: "value1A" }, 
 { name: "key1", value: "value1B" }, 
 { name: "key2", value: "value2" } 
]

```

 `Map.groupBy()` can combine these by the name: `Map.groupBy(array, (data) => data.name)` converts this into

```
Map { 
 key1 → [{ name: "key1", value: "value1A" }, { name: "key1", value: "value1B" }], 
 key2 → [{ name: "key2", value: "value2" }]
}

```

 `Map#entries()` into `Iterator#map` can convert it into a key-value pair where the value is an array:

```
const formFieldsToObject = (el) => {
 const groupedData = Map.groupBy(
 $(":input", el).serializeArray(),
 (data) => data.name
 )

 const entries = map
 .entries()
 .map(([key, group]) => [key, handleMultiValues(group)]);

 return Object.fromEntries(entries);
}

```

