I'm migrating a (.NET 10) project from CsvHelper 33.1 to 34.0.

 Using the migration guide I've updated a CSV-specific class, that's used only for writing a CSV file, from the following:

```
 public class CsvTargetFrameworkResult(TargetFrameworkResult targetFrameworkResult)
 {
 public string? ProjectName { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Project?.Name;
 public string? RepositoryName { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Repository?.Name;
 public string? Path { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Path;
 public string Version { get; set; } = string.Join(';', targetFrameworkResult.TargetFrameworks);
 }

```

 to:

```
 [CsvRecord]
 public partial class CsvTargetFrameworkResult(TargetFrameworkResult targetFrameworkResult)
 {
 public string? ProjectName { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Project?.Name;
 public string? RepositoryName { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Repository?.Name;
 public string? Path { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Path;
 public string Version { get; set; } = string.Join(';', targetFrameworkResult.TargetFrameworks);
 }

```

 However, I'm getting the following error:


 'CsvTargetFrameworkResult.targetFrameworkResult' has type 'Xxx.TargetFrameworkResult', which cannot be read from a CSV field; use a type with a static Parse method or add [CsvIgnore]

 I've tried refactoring this class to use an additional parameter-less constructor with a `[CsvConstructor]`, and a couple variations of trying to use `[CsvIgnore]`, but none of these attempts are allowing a build.

 How do I need to update this class for CsvHelper 34.x so that I can use it for writing a CSV file with `CsvWriter.WriteRecords(records)`?

 Example

 Project file:

```



 net10.0 
 enable 
 enable 








```

 Example.cs:

```
using CsvHelper;

namespace CsvHelperTestProj;

[CsvRecord]
public partial class CsvTargetFrameworkResult(TargetFrameworkResult targetFrameworkResult)
{
 public string? ProjectName { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Project?.Name;
 public string? RepositoryName { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Repository?.Name;
 public string? Path { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Path;
 public string Version { get; set; } = string.Join(';', targetFrameworkResult.TargetFrameworks);
}

public class TargetFrameworkResult
{
 public string? Path { get; set; }
 public Project? Project { get; set; }
 public Repository? Repository { get; set; }
 public List TargetFrameworks { get; set; } = [];
}

public class Project
{
 public string? Name { get; set; }
 public string? Id { get; set; }
}

public class Repository
{
 public string? Name { get; set; }
 public string? Id { get; set; }
 public string? Type { get; set; }
}

```

 Build error:


 Severity Code Description Project File Line Suppression State Details
Error (active) CSVH002 'CsvTargetFrameworkResult.targetFrameworkResult' has type 'CsvHelperTestProj.TargetFrameworkResult', which cannot be read from a CSV field; use a type with a static Parse method or add [CsvIgnore] CsvHelperTestProj X:...\Example.cs 6

solution:
As indicated in Migrating to v34.0.0 , CsvHelper supports deserializing types with parameterized constructors:


 More members are read : public and internal properties and fields that can be set, those of base classes too, and the parameters of the constructor .

 It does this by adding column(s) to its member map for every constructor parameter. [1] Thus, when generating CSV mapping code for `CsvTargetFrameworkResult`, CsvHelper will try to add column(s) for `targetFrameworkResult` and fail, because the type isn't marked with `[CsvRecord]`. The error message indicates a workaround:


 error CSVH002: 'CsvTargetFrameworkResult.targetFrameworkResult' has type 'CsvHelperTestProj.TargetFrameworkResult', which cannot be read from a CSV field; use a type with a static Parse method or add [CsvIgnore]

 However, the suggested workaround doesn't work. `[CsvIgnore]` can't be applied to a constructor parameter -- because, as of CsvHelper 34.0.0, the attribute can only target properties and fields.

 This looks to have been an oversight in CsvHelper 34.0.0; you might want to open an issue about this. In the meantime, if you want to continue to use primary constructors for your DTO types, the easiest workaround would seem to be to add a parameterless constructor, and mark it with `[CsvConstructor]` :

```
[CsvRecord]
public partial class CsvTargetFrameworkResult(TargetFrameworkResult targetFrameworkResult)
{
 [CsvConstructor]
 CsvTargetFrameworkResult() : this(new()) { } // Replace new() with the defaults you require

 public string? ProjectName { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Project?.Name;
 public string? RepositoryName { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Repository?.Name;
 public string? Path { get; set; } = targetFrameworkResult.Path;
 public string Version { get; set; } = string.Join(';', targetFrameworkResult.TargetFrameworks);
}

```

 Alternatively, you might consider replacing your current `class` implementation with a `record` type whose primary constructor includes the serializable properties, like so:

```
[CsvRecord]
[method: CsvConstructor] // Here "method:" targets the primary constructor
public partial record CsvTargetFrameworkResult(string? ProjectName, string? RepositoryName, string? Path, string Version = "")
{
 public CsvTargetFrameworkResult(TargetFrameworkResult targetFrameworkResult) : this(
 targetFrameworkResult.Project?.Name,
 targetFrameworkResult.Repository?.Name,
 targetFrameworkResult.Path,
 string.Join(';', targetFrameworkResult.TargetFrameworks)) {}
}

```

 This approach is, in my opinion, quite succinct and expressive.

 With either approach, a list of `CsvTargetFrameworkResult` objects can now be serialized using CsvWriter using a test .NET 10 console application.



 [1] To confirm this, consider the following simple type:

```
[CsvRecord]
public partial class Example2(string Value1In)
{
 public string Value1Out => Value1In + " output value";
}

```

 If I deserialize CSV corresponding to this and dump the results:

```
var csv = 
 """
 Value1In
 foo
 """;

using var reader = CsvReader.FromText(csv);
var list = reader.GetRecords ().ToList();

Console.WriteLine(System.Text.Json.JsonSerializer.Serialize(list));;

```

 The result will be `[{"Value1Out":"foo output value"}]`. So we can see that a column corresponding to the constructor parameter `Value1In` was added to CsvHelper's mapping.

