solution:
Here's how you check if a map contains a key.

```
val, ok := myMap["foo"]
// If the key exists
if ok {
 // Do something
}

```

 This initializes two variables. `val` is the value of "foo" from the map if it exists, or a "zero value" if it doesn't (in this case the empty string). `ok` is a `bool` that will be set to `true` if the key existed.

 If you want, you can shorten this to a one-liner.

```
if val, ok := myMap["foo"]; ok {
 //do something here
}

```

 Go allows you to put an initializing statement before the condition (notice the semicolon) in the if statement. The consequence of this is that the scope of`val` and `ok` will be limited to the body of the if statement, which is helpful if you only need to access them there.

code_example:
