In Nuxt, the "minification" effect is the default behavior of the Vue 3 Template Compiler .

 By default, the Vue 3 compiler uses an option called `whitespace: 'condense'`. This automatically removes newlines and condenses whitespace between HTML elements in your Vue templates to optimize performance and reduce the final bundle size.

 To prevent it, you need to configure the `whitespace` option of the Vue compiler to `'preserve'` in your `nuxt.config.ts`.

```
vite: {
 vue: {
 template: {
 compilerOptions: {
 whitespace: "preserve"
 }
 }
 },
 build: {
 minify: false,
 },
},

```

 A Note on the Document Shell

 Keep in mind that this solution applies to the content rendered by Vue (everything inside your components).

 The initial document shell (` ...`), which is generated by Nuxt's server engine (Nitro), is built using string concatenation and will inherently be output on a single line to save bytes.

 If you absolutely need the entire HTML file (including the ` `) to be pretty-printed, you will need to run a post-build formatting tool (such as Prettier ) over your `.output/public` directory after generation.

